    "content": "The Federal Government of Somalia has for the first time released salary payments to over 2,500 soldiers deployed in the Northeast region, marking a significant step in addressing long-standing grievances over unpaid wages. The payments were made overnight, according to officials familiar with the matter.\n\nIn addition to the salary disbursement, close to 200 officers have been promoted, with a particular focus on senior personnel from the 26th Division, which is slated to form the core of the region's formal security structure. The promotions and payments come amid ongoing efforts to build and reorganize the Northeast regional forces.\n\nThese developments are part of a wider initiative by the federal government to regularize military payroll and career progression, which have historically been hampered by logistical and financial constraints. The Northeast region, which includes areas under the Puntland administration, has long maintained its own parallel security forces, often operating with limited federal support.\n\nThe timing of the payments and promotions aligns with broader discussions between federal and regional authorities on integrating regional forces into the national security architecture. Such integration has been a key point of negotiation, with regional leaders seeking assurances on funding and command structures.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the move signals a potential improvement in the reliability of security institutions, which many diaspora members view as critical to stability and investment. Unpaid or irregularly paid soldiers have often been a source of insecurity, and the federal government's action may help restore confidence in its ability to manage security responsibilities.\n\nThe federal government has not yet issued a detailed statement on the total cost of the payments or the criteria used for the promotions. However, officials indicate that further similar measures are planned for other regions as part of the ongoing security sector reform.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/27/dfs-oo-mushaarka-u-fasaxday-in-ka-badan-2500-askari-oo-ku-sugan-waqooyi-bari/",
